# ============================================================
# flow/antenna_culled_partner.buda
#
# A segment whose PARTNER is CULLED out from under it — the
# rv/soc mechanism, isolated.  THIS IS AN OPEN DEFECT.
#
# DetailedNUTS runs three passes in this order:
#
#     place_by_layer  ->  adjust_bit_spans  ->  cull_keepout_crossers
#
# `adjust_bit_spans` extends each bit to reach its partner's
# track.  `cull_keepout_crossers` then deletes bits whose FINAL
# span still crosses a keepout.  A partner removed by that cull
# is removed AFTER the neighbour was extended to meet it — so
# the neighbour keeps metal aimed at a wire that no longer
# exists, and nothing retracts it.  #678's stale-end rule cannot
# help: it runs inside adjust_bit_spans, one pass too early.
#
# Measured here — seg 2's four bits culled, seg 1 left holding
# its full span out to x=710 where seg 2 used to be:
#
#   Bundle 1: Seg 1 bit 0 on layer M4 spans [183.5,710]
#             but this bit only reaches [183.5,183.5]
#             — 526.5 of dangling metal
#
#   4 findings, ~529 units each = 2115 units on M4.
#   detailed WL 2922, of which 2115 is metal reaching nothing.
#
# The AUDIT is fine — `check_design dnuts` reports every bit.
# It is the PLACER that leaves the metal.  The same shape at
# scale is flow/rv/soc: 2 bundles x 32 bits x 19,600 units.
#
# THE CONSTRUCTION IS THE INTERESTING PART, twice over.
#
# (1) A keepout declared UP FRONT is one the PLANNER routes
#     around, so it can never produce this.  Declaring it AFTER
#     `run_planner` is the deterministic stand-in for a
#     DEF-imported blockage, which is how rv/soc gets its.
#
# (2) The keepout must MISS the segment's span MIDPOINT.
#     `signal_tracks_in` tests a keepout at that single sample
#     point, so a keepout covering the midpoint fails placement
#     ADMISSION instead and the bits never reach the cull —
#     a different path with the opposite outcome, which is what
#     flow/antenna_starved_partner.buda holds.  That pair is the
#     evidence that the ORDERING is the cause and not the
#     keepout: same geometry, two ways to lose the partner, and
#     only the post-adjustment one dangles.
#
# The vehicle ends DIRTY on purpose (4 unplaced bits): culling a
# segment breaks the route, and that is the point of the setup.
# Do not add it to the QoR corpus.
#
# Run:  bin/buda flow/antenna_culled_partner.buda --no-viz
# ============================================================

def_layer 4 M4 H TOP 33.33
def_layer 5 M5 V TOP 33.33

corner_margin dx 10 dy 10

add_block drv  100 100  200 200
add_block rcv  700 600  800 700

add_bus b[4] drv.tx rcv.rx

run_bundler strict
generate_topologies

# Candidate 4 is Z_VHV@y400@x190 — V up from drv, H across, V
# down into rcv.  Pinned so the vehicle demonstrates ONE shape;
# without a pin the planner is free to pick an L and there is no
# middle segment to strand.  (The pin is by index, so a change to
# the candidate ORDER moves it — `dump_topologies` after
# `generate_topologies` shows the list.)
select_topology 1 4

run_planner 1
run_nuts

def_track_pattern 4 0  POWER 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4  GROUND 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4
def_track_pattern 5 0  POWER 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4  GROUND 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4

# Declared HERE, after the plan is committed: the planner never
# sees it, so seg2 keeps its assignment.
#
# seg2 is V on M5, spanning y 400..600 at x 710..790.  This
# keepout covers its tracks (x 600..900) and the LOW END of its
# span (y 380..450) while missing the midpoint y=500 — so
# admission succeeds at the midpoint sample, all four bits place,
# and the cull removes them afterwards on their final spans.
# Widen it to y 720 and the midpoint is covered: admission fails
# instead and the shape does not appear (see the starved twin).
add_keepout 600 380 900 450 5

run_detailed_nuts
dump_topologies --conn
check_design dnuts
report_wirelength
